{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01HHgU6vyMAzH6HNk3SXadwf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as a test bed. It derives formal specs from C source, generates Go implementations using AI, and verifies parity through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/<utility>/` - Go implementations of Unix utilities\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current State**: `pkg/testutils` is complete and in the source code, while `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/ts`, `cmd/wc`, and `cmd/cat` all have finished specs but their implementations are on merged branches rather than the current one.\n\n`cmd/sponge` is also spec-complete with code on merged branches, whereas `cmd/ls` and `cmd/du` still need their use cases and test suites written.\n\n**Completed work**: I've finished the differential testing harness for `pkg/testutils` and completed the measurement planning phase.\n\nI'm currently on rel01.0, which focuses on the `ts` command specification and differential testing, with the use case implementation still pending.\n\nLooking at the actual status, `cmd/ts` shows code hasn't started yet, even though the architecture references code at generation-merged tags on a different branch. The current branch is `generation-2026-02-26-20-39-40`, and `pkg/testutils` has already been implemented.\n\nSo the next step is implementing `cmd/ts` per prd004-ts. Given the constraint of only one task and the requirement to split implementation and tests separately, I need to prioritize—the most critical piece would be the types and interfaces for the `ts` command.\n\nSince the types and interfaces are straightforward enough to combine with the implementation within the line budget, I should propose a single implementation task that covers both. The PRD specifies six requirements: default behavior, custom format strings, incremental mode, elapsed-since-start mode, monotonic clock mode, and relative timing. With only one task allowed, the implementation task combining types and interfaces is the right choice to move rel01.0-uc001-ts forward. -time conversion mode (-r)\n- R7: Exit codes and error handling\n- R8: Environment\n- R9: Differential testing\n\nThe test suite is already specified (test-rel01.0 with 12 test cases covering all requirements).\n\nI need to decide between implementation-first or tests-first, but the constraint is clear: implementation comes before tests. So I'll propose the cmd/ts implementation task covering R1-R8, with the differential tests as a separate follow-up.\n\nFor the implementation, I need to create a Go version of the original Perl script. The main file would handle all the core functionality: reading from stdin, applying timestamps with the default format or custom format strings, and supporting the various modes like incremental timing, elapsed time since start, monotonic clock, and relative time conversion. This covers the key requirements around timestamp formatting, mode selection, and proper exit code handling.\n\nThe design approach relies on Go's built-in time package—specifically using `time.Now()` which automatically provides monotonic clock readings for the -m mode requirement, and handling timezone via the TZ environment variable through Go's standard time parsing.\n\nThe main challenge is that Go's `time.Format` uses its own format syntax rather than strftime, so I'll need to implement a conversion layer to translate the strftime format strings from the PRD into Go's format directives. Looking at the line count estimates for each component—the main loop, timestamp parsing mode, and subsecond handling—I'm getting around 320 lines total, which is slightly over the 250-350 target but still reasonable. I think the implementation task is the right place to start since we need the core functionality before testing, so I'll propose that as the first task. I'm laying out the requirements for the ts command implementation, including the default mode that reads stdin and prepends timestamps, a custom format string mode supporting strftime conversions and ts-specific subsecond extensions, and an incremental mode that tracks elapsed time between lines with mutual exclusivity constraints.\n\nNow I'm looking at the remaining modes: elapsed-since-start tracking with monotonic increasing values, leveraging Go's built-in monotonic clock support, and a relative-time conversion mode that recognizes and transforms various timestamp patterns like syslog and ISO-8601 formats.\n\nI also need to handle exit codes properly, respect the TZ environment variable for timezone handling, and ensure the parsing dependency for relative-time conversion is always available.\n\nNow I'm working through the design decisions, starting with how to translate strftime format strings into Go's time.Format layout strings, handling all the standard specifiers plus some subsecond extensions that need preprocessing. For elapsed time formatting with the -i and -s flags...\n\nSetting the timezone to GMT before formatting ensures the delta-second value produces the correct elapsed string, then I'll restore the original timezone afterward. The monotonic clock handling for -m is straightforward since Go's time.Now() already includes that reading automatically. For relative mode, I need to recognize timestamps using regex patterns, parse them into time.Time objects with multiple layout attempts, calculate how long ago they occurred, and format the output as a duration followed by \"ago\" using the most appropriate time unit.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the acceptance criteria to ensure the implementation meets all requirements. I'm estimating the implementation will land around 300-370 lines total—the strftime translation function alone is substantial with all the format conversions, and there's complexity in the relative mode parsing and duration formatting. This is slightly above the 250-350 target, but it feels like the natural scope for a self-contained utility, so I'll proceed with this approach rather than splitting it further. strftime out into a helper file but that would be over-engineering.\n\nActually, re-reading P2: \"Code tasks should target the line range specified in the CONSTRAINTS section of the prompt (typically 250-350 lines of production code), touching no more than 5-7 files.\"\n\nThe constraints section says: \"Do NOT create tasks larger than 350 lines of production code. Target 250-350 lines per task.\"\n\nThis is a hard constraint at 350. The ts implementation might exceed this. But let me think through the approach: I can implement strftime more compactly using a map lookup with replacement, and the -r mode for relative dates, while complex, should fit within the limit if done efficiently. A skilled implementer could probably complete this in around 300-350 lines. Since I can only create one task and the ts utility is essential for rel01.0, I'll keep it as a single task.\n\nI should also verify the file structure—the source would go in `cmd/ts/main.go` since binaries live in `bin/` but the source is organized under `cmd/`. Let me finalize the YAML with the correct file path and task description.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":77477,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":77477},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aff54926-4144-490d-a88f-3499ad4a88d2","uuid":"817806f3-0930-4db2-85f6-e6cc9c70263f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01HHgU6vyMAzH6HNk3SXadwf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Specification-driven development using Unix utilities as a test bed — derive formal specs from C source, generate Go implementations via AI, verify parity through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four main components: `pkg/testutils` (differential harness), `pkg/sys` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format` (output formatting), `cmd/<utility>/` (utility implementations). A mage-based build drives code generation via the `mage-claude-orchestrator`.\n\n**Current State**:\n- `pkg/testutils` — **complete** (source code present in project context)\n- `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/ts`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge` — specs complete, code exists only at generation-merged tags (not on current branch)\n- `cmd/ls`, `cmd/du` — PRDs complete, use cases and test suites pending\n\n**Current Release**: `rel01.0` — one use case: `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (in_progress, code not started on current branch).\n\n**Next Task**: Implement `cmd/ts/main.go` — the only blocking item for rel01.0. Tests are a separate follow-on task; per P2, implementation comes first.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/ts: Implement timestamp stdin lines (prd004-ts R1-R8)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd004-ts.yaml (full PRD -- all requirements, acceptance criteria, non-goals)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (harness contract so implementation matches test expectations)\n      - pkg/testutils/normalize.go (TimestampNormalizer patterns for context)\n      - go.mod (module path: github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ts/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Complete ts implementation: flag parsing, strftime-to-Go translation, stdin processing loop, -i/-s/-m/-r modes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default mode (prd004-ts R1): read stdin line by line (newline-delimited); prepend each line with a timestamp formatted via the default strftime format '%b %d %H:%M:%S' and a single space; flush stdout after each line; preserve the original newline; pass through partial lines at EOF with a timestamp prefix; exit 0 on clean EOF; do not exit non-zero on SIGPIPE.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement custom format string (prd004-ts R2): accept an optional positional argument as a strftime format string; support all standard strftime(3) conversion specifications by translating them to Go time.Format layout tokens; support ts-specific subsecond extensions %.S (seconds.microseconds, e.g. '32.001234'), %.s (Unix epoch.microseconds), %.T (HH:MM:SS.microseconds) by pre-processing them before calling time.Format, using a single time.Now() sample per line for atomicity (prd004-ts R2.4).\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement incremental mode (prd004-ts R3.1-R3.4): -i shows elapsed time since the previous line (first line shows elapsed since start); default format '%H:%M:%S' with TZ=GMT so strftime on a delta-second value produces a correct elapsed string; a custom format argument overrides the default; -i and -s are mutually exclusive -- passing both must print a usage error to stderr and exit non-zero.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement elapsed-since-start mode (prd004-ts R4.1-R4.3): -s shows elapsed time since ts started, increasing monotonically; default format '%H:%M:%S' with TZ=GMT; a custom format argument overrides the default.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement monotonic clock mode (prd004-ts R5.1-R5.3): -m uses time.Now() which already includes a monotonic reading in Go; -m is compatible with all format modes including -i, -s, and subsecond extensions; no special clock-source change is required -- document this invariant in a comment.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement relative-time conversion mode (prd004-ts R6.1-R6.5): -r scans each input line for the four recognized timestamp patterns from prd004-ts R6.2 (syslog 'Jan  5 14:30:00', ISO-8601 '2024-01-05T14:30:00.000Z', RFC 2822 '16 Jun 94 07:29:35 GMT', lastlog 'Mon Jan  5 14:30') and replaces each match with a human-readable relative age string (e.g., '15m5s ago'); when -r and a format argument are both given, convert matched timestamps to the specified strftime format instead of a relative string; lines with no recognized timestamp pass through unchanged; -r is mutually exclusive with -i and -s.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement exit codes and error handling (prd004-ts R7.1-R7.3): exit 0 on clean EOF from stdin; exit non-zero and print a usage message to stderr on unrecognized flag; document in a comment that the -r parsing library is always compiled in (no runtime-missing condition possible in Go).\"\n      - id: R8\n        text: \"Implement environment handling (prd004-ts R8.1-R8.2): respect the TZ environment variable for wall-clock timestamps in default and custom-format modes; use TZ=GMT internally when formatting elapsed-time values for -i and -s modes, regardless of the user's TZ setting, matching the Perl source behavior.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"strftime translation: implement a strftime(format string, t time.Time) string function that converts strftime format specifiers to Go time.Format layout strings and calls t.Format. Cover at minimum the specifiers used by ts: %b, %d, %H, %M, %S, %Y, %m, %e, %j, %Z, %z, %%, %n, %t, %p, %I, %T, %F, %R. For unrecognized specifiers, pass them through unchanged. Pre-process %.S, %.s, %.T before the main substitution loop since they contain a dot that would otherwise conflict with standard specifiers (prd004-ts R2.3).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Elapsed-time formatting for -i and -s (prd004-ts R3.2, R4.2): to format a duration as HH:MM:SS via strftime, convert the duration to a time.Time anchored at the Unix epoch (time.Unix(int64(seconds), 0).UTC()) and format it with TZ=GMT. Do not temporarily mutate the TZ environment variable at runtime; instead, call time.Unix(...).UTC() and use time.Format directly with the Go equivalent of '%H:%M:%S' ('15:04:05').\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Relative-mode duration formatting (prd004-ts R6.1): format elapsed age as a compact string using the largest applicable unit -- days (Xd), hours (Xh), minutes (Xm), seconds (Xs) -- concatenated in descending order, followed by ' ago'. Zero values for the leading unit are omitted. Examples: 15m5s ago, 2h30m ago, 3d4h ago.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Flag mutual exclusivity (prd004-ts R3.4, R6.5): after parsing flags with the flag package, check that at most one of -i, -s, -r is active. If -i and -s are both set, or if -r is set alongside -i or -s, print a usage message to stderr via fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...) and os.Exit(1).\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"'go build -o bin/ts ./cmd/ts/' completes with no errors and produces bin/ts.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"printf 'line1\\\\nline2\\\\nline3\\\\n' | ./bin/ts produces three lines each prefixed with a timestamp matching the pattern '<Mon> <DD> <HH:MM:SS>' followed by a space and the original line content, and exits 0.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | ./bin/ts -s produces three lines with HH:MM:SS elapsed timestamps that are monotonically non-decreasing, and exits 0.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"printf 'first\\\\nsecond\\\\nthird\\\\n' | ./bin/ts -i produces three lines with per-line HH:MM:SS incremental timestamps, and exits 0.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"printf '' | ./bin/ts (empty stdin) produces no output and exits 0. Same for -s and -i modes.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"printf 'partial' | ./bin/ts (no trailing newline) produces exactly one line with a timestamp prefix and 'partial' -- no extra newline is appended.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"./bin/ts -z exits non-zero and writes a usage message to stderr.\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"./bin/ts -i -s exits non-zero and writes a usage error to stderr.\"\n      - id: AC9\n        text: \"./bin/ts '%H:%M:%S' with a single stdin line produces a line prefixed with a valid HH:MM:SS timestamp, confirming the custom format argument path works.\"\n      - id: AC10\n        text: \"golangci-lint run ./cmd/ts/... reports no warnings or errors.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":77477,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":77477},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aff54926-4144-490d-a88f-3499ad4a88d2","uuid":"59e16d18-229d-4b5e-9fc9-81669e9e12d8"}
